# Phase 18 MessageDecoder buffer cursor parity

Recorded: 2026-10-24 08:34 UTC
Branch: phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure
Code commit: b2326a3a fix(common): preserve message decoder buffer cursors
Pinned Java reference: RocketMQ 5.5.1 at efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9

## Change

The public `MessageDecoder.createMessageId(ByteBuffer, ByteBuffer, long)` Rust
mapping now uses `Cursor` inputs to preserve output flip/reset, source/output
positions, bytes returned from the backing array, and the Java put failure
order. Its invoked fixture covers IPv4/IPv6, a positioned source/output pair,
a short address, output capacity failure, address-put overflow and offset-put
overflow.

`countInnerMsgNum(ByteBuffer)` now maps to a mutable cursor and advances to its
limit on success. The fixture covers nonzero start, truncated size prefix,
incomplete positive size, and a positive size below four followed by Java's
next read/position failure. Negative size at position zero preserves the
Java exception boundary and cursor position. Rust returns a bounded error for
zero and negative sizes because Java can loop without progress or rewind.

